Subject: [PATCH 09/15] WP-0.7, enforcement (cld-advisor's series 5, patch 9;
 first built on 5c7abd72a; built again on main's bf76e5ab1 with the 41 patches
 of R80b, the ten patches of series 4, the move of the old front pages and the
 first eight of series 5 applied): A TABLE OF WHAT IS HELD BY NAME SHORTENS
 FREELY AND GROWS ONLY BY A RECORDED ACT (main's request of 2026-11-12, made
 with the landing of series 3; its M10). When a rule comes, what already
 breaks it is held by name in a table beside the rule, as found, and the table
 may only shrink. That was said in a comment, and here and there held by a
 count in a lock. A name added to such a table excuses what its rule was made
 to refuse, and nothing read the table's history. THE MEND: nine tables are
 named in one list, HELD_TABLES: six in the gates, two in the retraction
 sweep, one in the root conftest. Each is read along main's own line, from the
 rule's anchor or from the commit in which the table first stood, and the file
 as it is on the disk last. A name that enters a table after that needs a
 record under the table's name in ENTERED_BY_NAME, with its date and why; a
 name that leaves and comes back has entered again and needs a second. A table
 is read as it is written: one plain statement at the top level of its file
 binds it to a table written out, and nothing in the file binds the name again
 or changes the table in place. A table bound twice, widened by a second
 statement, shadowed by a parameter or a local name where it is read, or built
 by anything but a table written out, cannot be read and fails the gate. For
 the six tables of the gates' own file the gate also compares what it holds in
 memory with what is written. A table named as these are, ending in _AS_FOUND
 or _BY_NAME, that stands in the gates, in a checker or in the root conftest
 and is not in the list fails the gate. The rule is asked by the gate on the
 gates' own controls, which reads the past already: it is not a gate of its
 own, so the count of gates stays 53; main may want it apart. The record is a
 table of its own beside the list, and not RAISED_BY_NAME: that table stands
 three lines under LEFT_BY_NAME, where main writes its records, and a patch
 that adds a name with its record would not apply once main has written there.
 ON MAIN: since the anchor one name has entered one of the nine after it first
 stood, a file added to FRAMING_EXEMPT by the first patch of series 3, held as
 found on the day the gate first read a .json. Its record is written in this
 patch, from that patch's own comment; it is main's to correct. One name has
 left: main shortened STANDS_AS_FOUND at the landing of series 3. A passing
 gate that holds a record prints its sentence at every push, so the line of
 the controls gate now names that file. The reading takes four tenths of a
 second: the gates' file has stood in 33 states since the anchor, and the cost
 grows with that number. WHAT IT COSTS, for main to weigh: as with a ceiling,
 a name that entered in a commit is not taken back by taking it out in a later
 commit; it is recorded. And a series that widens a rule and holds what it
 finds must write the record in the same patch: the series after this one
 does. WHAT IT DOES NOT REACH: a table of what is excused under any other
 name. Thirteen more stand in the gates and two checkers: the lists of exempt
 files and prefixes, and the sets held since before these rules. None has
 grown since the anchor, so holding them would need no record today, and each
 would be one line of the list: main's to say. The code that reads a table,
 which can be changed to excuse more than the table names. A table changed
 from another file as that file is loaded, for the three tables outside the
 gates' own file: the lock compares those in memory, the gate does not. The
 lock: nine tests added to tests/test_a_gate_reads_all_it_is_about.py. On the
 commit before, all nine fail; the four that plant in a copy of the gates fail
 with the controls gate saying ok: a name added to a table of the gates, a row
 excused from the sweep by name, a name added in memory as the gates are
 loaded, a new table in no list. Tested against planted errors; computed once.
 0 of 19.

